{
  "id": "2026-10-15-go-connection-reuse",
  "title": "Go 1.27 quietly makes many HTTP clients five times faster",
  "topic": "테크",
  "level": 3,
  "voice": "en-US-AndrewMultilingualNeural",
  "sentences": [
    "Good morning!",
    "Today's story is about a small change in Go that could make a big difference.",
    "With HTTP/1, a client can only reuse a connection after it has read the whole response.",
    "But many programs don't bother reading the body.",
    "For example, a health check only cares about the status code.",
    "Up to now, Go threw that connection away and opened a new one.",
    "Every new connection means another round trip for the TCP and TLS handshakes.",
    "This kind of slowdown tends to go unnoticed, because nothing actually breaks.",
    "In Go 1.27, the HTTP client now drains unread bodies in the background, for a short time.",
    "That way, the connection can go back into the pool.",
    "In the Go team's own test, a hundred requests went from about seven hundred milliseconds to about one hundred fifty.",
    "That's roughly five times faster, and you don't have to change a single line of code.",
    "The team also says you can drop the manual workarounds people used to write.",
    "Just close the body, and let the library handle the rest.",
    "If your services still talk HTTP/1 to each other, this one is worth an upgrade.",
    "Try explaining today, in your own words, why closing a response early used to be slow."
  ],
  "summary_ko": [
    "HTTP/1 에서는 응답 본문을 끝까지 읽어야 연결을 재사용할 수 있는데, Go 1.26 까지는 다 안 읽고 닫으면 연결을 버리고 새로 열었다.",
    "Go 1.27 은 안 읽은 본문을 백그라운드에서 잠깐(최대 50ms·256KB) 비워서 연결을 풀에 돌려보낸다.",
    "Go 팀 테스트에서 요청 100건이 약 710ms → 150ms 로 5배 빨라졌고, 코드는 고칠 필요가 없다."
  ],
